returns_fraud_signals::challenger::v1.text:
{
  "findings": [
    {
      "question": "claims_supported",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "Most load-bearing claims hold (no internal measurement, empty abuse field, no reason code, Orlin's 24-month prerequisite). Two do not. (1) C2 says the audit's 'scope and price are known rather than estimated', and the support statement calls it 'a known, one-off $60,000 scope'. The memo says the opposite: it was an 'Estimated cost' for a request submitted in March 2026 and never scheduled — no vendor quote, no labour line, and C11 concedes the labour is unquantified. Calling an estimate 'known' is the single most consequential misread in the pack, because the whole case is that this option is cheap and bounded. (2) C6 asserts a post-refund condition audit is 'the one form of scrutiny governance clearly permits'. The policy clause cited says only that inspection must be triggered by a property of the item or transaction; it says nothing about post-refund audits, and by the draft's own reading the same clause equally permits ALT-5 item-triggered inspection (which ALT-5 describes as 'the one control governance explicitly contemplates'). That is an inference presented as a governance finding, and the two statements cannot both be true. Separately, the proposed test promises a rate that will 'accept or reject the 5.9% benchmark assumption', but the benchmark is 5.9% of returned merchandise *value*; a unit-level condition-inspection rate is a different quantity and cannot accept or reject it.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-6d6576ca",
          "quote": "Estimated cost was $60,000 for a single-quarter sample across eight stores and the returns hub."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-6d6576ca",
          "quote": "The estimate applies the Specialty Retail Council's 2024 published estimate that 5.9% of\nreturned merchandise value is attributable to abusive returns to Alderwick's FY26 refund\ntotal of $71.2M."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-829d33e0",
          "quote": "Where an inspection is required, it must be triggered by a property of the item or\nthe transaction, not by a characterisation of the customer."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-8f056b74",
          "quote": "FY27 adds no headcount to loss prevention."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "contradictions_considered",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "The big conflicts are engaged well: the $4.2M benchmark-versus-measurement conflict, the return-rate-as-abuse-proxy conflict, the mix alternative, the Orlin data prerequisite, and the FY26/FY27 refund-base restatement all appear in claims or conditions. Two flagged conflicts are then re-committed rather than resolved. First, a condition states the sample must 'answer the online-versus-store question that loss prevention says it has never been able to look at' — but the pack's own contradiction record shows channel-level return rates already exist (online 21.0% vs store 9.7%); the gap that genuinely does not exist is per-customer cross-channel linkage, which a unit-level audit will not create either. Repeating loss prevention's merged claim as a design requirement risks scoping the sample to answer a question that is already answered. Second, ALT-1's risk line repeats leadership's 'six-quarter rise', which the contradiction log shows is unsupported by the retained series (six observations, five increases, starting 2025-Q1 at 11.2).",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-0560101f",
          "quote": "My instinct is that the online channel is far worse than the stores, but I have never been\nable to look."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-47808bde",
          "quote": "value: 21.0"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-7f296027",
          "quote": "value: 9.7"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-a48bba41",
          "quote": "value: 11.2"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-0560101f",
          "quote": "Return rate is the number I watch. It has gone up six quarters running. That is abuse, and\nI do not need a study to tell me so."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "preference_as_evidence",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "The draft handles stakeholder preference unusually well where it belongs to others: the Chief Merchant's conviction and the vendor's payback case are both explicitly named as preference rather than finding, and the two-manager anecdote base is stated plainly. The problem is the author's own judgements carried as constraints. C6 is labelled governance_constraint but is an inference about what a policy permits, not a policy statement. C10 is labelled business_constraint but its operative content — that Tarnside is 'the strongest targeting signal in the pack' — is an assessment; the audit it rests on explicitly disclaims sizing. C9 is labelled fact but its second half ('which is why a measured baseline should precede any further restriction') is the recommendation, not the record. None of these is dishonest, but a reader scanning claim types will treat three opinions as established constraints.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-5a5c4c77",
          "quote": "The audit did not weight SKUs by sales volume, and no link was made between the affected\nSKUs and actual return volumes."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-829d33e0",
          "quote": "A return may not be refused, restricted, or fee-charged on the basis of an automated score\nwithout documented human review and a stated appeal path."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "non_ai_considered",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "The recommendation is itself non-AI, and the non-AI field is broad (hold, defer, instrument, inspect, UX, partner, rules). But the nearest substitute is not compared on the dimension that decides between them. ALT-5 item-triggered condition inspection on the Tarnside line would produce a condition measurement *and* recovery, under the same governance clause the draft relies on, and the draft's own conditions concede the audit 'produces no financial recovery on its own'. ALT-5 is set aside because no abuse rate is measured — which is circular, since inspecting is how one would measure. The real trade is customer-experience risk and refusal record-keeping versus one lost quarter, and that trade is asserted rather than argued. Likewise ALT-7 is called the strongest evidence in the pack and then routed to a separate sponsorship decision without any comparison of expected effect against $60,000 of audit.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-829d33e0",
          "quote": "Where a return is refused, the record of the refusal and its stated reason must be retained\nfor 24 months and must be producible on request by the customer."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-d237cb24",
          "quote": "share_of_returns_percent: 58.0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "no_build_considered",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "ALT-1 (hold) and ALT-2 (defer) are both present with full dimension assessments, and the chosen option is research rather than build, so the spirit is respected. The gap is sequencing within the recommendation itself. Rider (b) — the category-mix decomposition — is described as available at zero marginal cost, and 'what would change it' concedes that a mix-dominated result would reframe the problem as assortment and fit and shrink the audit to a smaller confirmatory sample. If that is true, the free analysis is a gate on the $60,000, not a parallel task. Funding both in one decision spends the money before the cheapest test that could redirect it has reported. The pack also lists 'What is the value of confirmed_abuse_cases?' as still missing evidence — a one-hour query that could partly moot the audit — yet it is placed under 'what would change it' rather than done first.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-2d12e461",
          "quote": "field added to the returns dashboard in 2026-Q2 and never populated; there is no case management system in which an abuse case could be opened"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-08a56377",
          "quote": "value: 31.0"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-53319f96",
          "quote": "value: 46.0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "overconfident",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "'Moderate' is generous once the caveats the draft itself lists are taken at face value. The diagnosis (nothing has been measured) is strongly evidenced. The prescription is not: there is no sampling design, no power calculation, no rubric, no inter-rater evidence, no time-and-motion figure, an eight-of-118-store frame generalised by assumption, a cost that the source calls an estimate rather than a price, and a target quantity (unit condition) that is not the quantity in the benchmark it claims to adjudicate (share of returned merchandise value). The pack also contains no evidence that returned units are physically retrievable at store level after refund, which the post-refund sampling condition requires. A research option whose central deliverable — a decision-grade rate — has zero supporting evidence in the pack should be carried at low, with the design questions answered before funding.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-6d6576ca",
          "quote": "No internal measurement of abusive returns exists."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-6d6576ca",
          "quote": "Estimated cost was $60,000 for a single-quarter sample across eight stores and the returns hub."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-8f056b74",
          "quote": "Hold returns processing cost per unit flat or lower."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "what_could_make_it_wrong",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "Four specific ways this is a mistake, none of them generic. (1) Condition is not abuse. Worn, used or damaged units also arrive through quality_or_defect (14.0% of returns) and damaged_in_transit (7.0%), and the approved transit-packaging change exists precisely because hardgoods damage claims exceeded target for three quarters. An audit that scores 'unsellable condition' will hand leadership a number that overstates abuse, and the recommendation asks leadership to pre-accept it as 'the number of record before it is produced'. A pre-committed wrong number is worse than the benchmark it replaces. (2) Unit-versus-value mismatch: the 5.9% benchmark is a share of returned merchandise value, so a unit-condition rate cannot accept or reject it, and the audit will not settle the argument it is being funded to settle. (3) Sampling feasibility: post-refund sampling at eight stores presumes returned units are still identifiable and retrievable after refund; nothing in the pack establishes that, and if it fails the design collapses into inspection at receipt, i.e. ALT-5, with its customer risk. (4) Cost: $60,000 is a March-2026 estimate with inspection labour excluded, on a team given no headcount and a flat cost-per-unit target — the audit can be pulled mid-quarter and yield an unusable partial sample.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-d849ba4c",
          "quote": "reason_code: quality_or_defect"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-9c3a6615",
          "quote": "reason_code: damaged_in_transit"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-3d2cd42c",
          "quote": "transit damage claims on hardgoods exceeded target in three consecutive\nquarters"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-6d6576ca",
          "quote": "The estimate applies the Specialty Retail Council's 2024 published estimate that 5.9% of\nreturned merchandise value is attributable to abusive returns to Alderwick's FY26 refund\ntotal of $71.2M."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "what_to_test",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "The stated test asserts the outcome it needs ('a confidence interval narrow enough') without any design that would produce it. Before $60,000 is committed: (a) run the zero-cost mix decomposition and query confirmed_abuse_cases first, and treat a mix-dominated result as a stop condition, not a footnote; (b) prove the sampling frame with a 150-300 unit dry run at the hub only, confirming that refunded units can be located, matched to their reason code, and scored; (c) write the rubric and double-score that dry run to report inter-rater agreement, separating consumer wear from supplier defect and transit damage, since the audit's number will otherwise be uninterpretable; (d) produce a per-unit time-and-motion figure and test it against the flat cost-per-unit target before store labour is committed; (e) fix the estimand — value-weighted or unit-weighted — and pre-register the precision target, because the benchmark being tested is value-based; (f) get Legal's written position on recording reason-code discordance against a transaction, since purchase and return history is personal data; (g) obtain leadership's written acceptance of the estimand and its confidence interval before sampling, given the Chief Merchant's stated position that no study is needed.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-829d33e0",
          "quote": "Customer purchase and return history is personal data."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-0560101f",
          "quote": "That is abuse, and\nI do not need a study to tell me so."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-7f0a7541",
          "quote": "no reason code exists for suspected abuse and an associate has no field in which to record one"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "reclassify": [
    {
      "claim_id": "C2",
      "new_type": "assumption",
      "reason": "Labelled fact and used to argue the price is 'known rather than estimated'. The source memo says 'Estimated cost was $60,000' for a request that was never scheduled, and C11 concedes the inspection labour is unquantified. Cost certainty is an assumption, and it is the assumption doing most of the work in the recommendation."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "C6",
      "new_type": "assumption",
      "reason": "Labelled governance_constraint, but the policy only states that inspection must be triggered by a property of the item or transaction. That a post-refund condition audit is 'the one form of scrutiny governance clearly permits' — and that it carries no written-reason or 24-month retention burden — is the author's inference and needs Legal confirmation before it is presented as policy."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "C10",
      "new_type": "assumption",
      "reason": "Labelled business_constraint. The remit point is a genuine constraint, but the operative content — that Tarnside fit is 'the strongest targeting signal in the pack' — is an unsized assessment; the size-chart audit explicitly did not weight SKUs by sales or link them to return volumes."
    }
  ],
  "recommended_support": "low",
  "what_would_change_it": [
    "A dry-run sample confirming that refunded units are physically retrievable and matchable to their reason codes at store level, plus a reported inter-rater agreement figure on a double-scored subsample — without this the audit's central deliverable is unevidenced.",
    "A restated estimand: a value-weighted abuse measure with a pre-registered precision target, so the output is comparable to the 5.9%-of-returned-value benchmark it claims to test rather than a different quantity.",
    "A rubric that separates consumer wear from supplier defect and transit damage, given quality_or_defect at 14.0% and damaged_in_transit at 7.0% of returns and the approved packaging change; without it a 'condition' rate will be read as an abuse rate.",
    "A per-unit inspection time-and-motion figure showing the labour can be absorbed on a headcount-flat team against the flat cost-per-unit target, and a refreshed price rather than the March 2026 estimate.",
    "The result of the free mix decomposition and the value of confirmed_abuse_cases, both obtainable before any spend; a mix-dominated result should stop or shrink the audit rather than run alongside it.",
    "Legal's written position that per-unit condition and reason-code discordance capture creates no customer-facing record obligation."
  ],
  "what_to_test": [
    "Category-mix decomposition of the 11.2%→14.8% rise, and a query of confirmed_abuse_cases, both before any funding decision — treat a mix-dominated result as a stop condition.",
    "Hub-only dry run of 150-300 refunded units to prove the sampling frame: can units be located post-refund, matched to reason code, and scored at all.",
    "Double-scoring of that dry run against a written rubric, reporting inter-rater agreement and the rate of ambiguous cases between consumer wear, supplier defect and transit damage.",
    "Time-and-motion measurement of minutes per inspected unit, converted to cost per unit and tested against the flat processing-cost target.",
    "Power/precision calculation for the eight-store-plus-hub frame, stated on the value-weighted estimand, showing what interval $60,000 actually buys.",
    "Legal review of the capture step (condition, reason-code discordance) against the personal-data and record-keeping clauses.",
    "Written pre-acceptance from the Chief Merchant of the estimand and its confidence interval, given the stated position that no study is required."
  ]
}

